<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  sp11-boottrace.wprp - Windows boot trace of platform bring-up on the
  Surface Pro 11 (X1E80100), for diffing against a Linux boot of the same event.

  WHY THIS EXISTS

  The camera case for tracing Windows was rejected on evidence: neither
  qccamisp8380.sys nor qccammipicsi8380.sys logs sensor register writes, and the
  register tables were recovered statically from the sensor module blob anyway.

  USB4 and Type-C are the opposite. The providers below are Microsoft-authored
  and manifest-based, so they decode with no TMF and no Ghidra. Windows works
  with a Thunderbolt dock and Linux does no USB4 at all, so there is a real
  behavioural gap to diff rather than a confirmation exercise. And the boot
  window is where our ALTMODE_PAN_EN race lives - fatal at 6 s, harmless at
  66 s - which is currently papered over with pmic_glink_altmode.pan_enable=0
  plus a deferral service.

  NO BITLOCKER RISK

  wpr -addboot uses the Autologger registry mechanism, NOT the boot
  configuration database. Nothing here touches bcdedit, so it does not trip
  BitLocker recovery. (Kernel debugging would; do not go that route casually on
  this machine.)

  USAGE - from an ELEVATED prompt

      wpr -addboot tools\sp11-boottrace.wprp!SP11Platform -filemode
      shutdown /r /t 0
      ...boot, and if tracing a dock, have it attached or plug it in early...
      wpr -stopboot %USERPROFILE%\Documents\sp11-boot.etl "dock cold boot"

  WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE - verified by an arm/cancel round trip, 2026-08-09

  -addboot prints NOTHING on success. What it actually does is create two
  registry keys, and those are the only reliable evidence it worked:

      HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\WMI\Autologger\
          WPR_initiated_WprApp_boottr_SP11 Platform Collector    Start = 1
          WPR_initiated_WprApp_boottr_WPR System Collector       Start = 1

  TRAP: while armed, `wpr -status` reports "WPR is not recording". That is
  correct - the session does not start until the next boot - but it reads like
  the arm failed, and arming twice is the obvious wrong response. Check the
  registry key, not -status.

  Confirmed at the same time: bcdedit /enum {current} shows no debug or bootlog
  flags after -addboot. The boot configuration really is untouched.

  CANCELLING - just an elevated prompt, no recovery media, no reboot:

      wpr -cancelboot
          "Boottrace is scheduled but has not started. Canceling the Autologger."

  Arming a boot trace CANNOT make the machine unbootable; it is an ETW session,
  not a boot setting. The worst case is a slow boot and a large file. So
  -cancelboot is not an emergency tool - its real job is stopping the trace from
  re-arming on every subsequent boot, because the Autologger persists until
  stopped. If you somehow could not log in at all, the deep version is deleting
  the two registry keys above, which from WinRE means loading the SYSTEM hive.

  SIZE: the default output lands in %TEMP% on C:. A full boot with six providers
  plus the system keywords gets big; use -recordtempto <path> to put it
  somewhere with room. -filemode is not optional for a full boot.

  READING IT

      wpa.exe sp11-boot.etl            (Windows Performance Analyzer)
      tracerpt sp11-boot.etl -o dump.xml -of XML       (no-GUI text dump)

  The Linux-side counterpart, for a comparable trace: initcall_debug plus
  trace_event= on the kernel command line, with ramoops to survive a bad boot.
  This board already has a ramoops node.
-->
<WindowsPerformanceRecorder Version="1.0" Author="arm64-egpu" Comments="SP11 platform bring-up">
  <Profiles>

    <SystemCollector Id="SystemCollector_SP11" Name="NT Kernel Logger">
      <BufferSize Value="1024" />
      <Buffers Value="512" />
    </SystemCollector>

    <EventCollector Id="EventCollector_SP11" Name="SP11 Platform Collector">
      <BufferSize Value="1024" />
      <Buffers Value="512" />
    </EventCollector>

    <!-- Driver load order and device init are the point; Power and Interrupt
         are here because the Type-C/alt-mode work is a power-sequencing race. -->
    <SystemProvider Id="SystemProvider_SP11">
      <Keywords>
        <Keyword Value="Loader" />
        <Keyword Value="ProcessThread" />
        <Keyword Value="Drivers" />
        <Keyword Value="Power" />
        <Keyword Value="Interrupt" />
        <Keyword Value="DPC" />
        <!-- The USB/Type-C stack is KMDF, so its work lands here. -->
        <Keyword Value="WDFInterrupt" />
        <Keyword Value="WDFDPC" />
      </Keywords>
    </SystemProvider>

    <!-- GUIDs verified against `logman query providers` on this machine,
         2026-08-09. Named rather than trusted from memory. -->

    <!-- Type-C connector manager: alt-mode entry/exit, partner negotiation.
         The closest Windows analogue to our pmic_glink altmode path. -->
    <EventProvider Id="EP_UcmUcsiCx"   Name="569D11AA-5068-5EE5-DA22-CE541C0B1481" Level="5" />

    <!-- USB4 router: discovery and tunnel setup. Linux does none of this today
         because parade,disable-usb4 is set on both retimers. -->
    <EventProvider Id="EP_Usb4Router"  Name="D07E8C3F-78FB-4C22-B77C-2203D00BFDF3" Level="5" />

    <EventProvider Id="EP_UsbHub3"     Name="AC52AD17-CC01-4F85-8DF5-4DCE4333C99B" Level="5" />
    <EventProvider Id="EP_UsbXhci"     Name="30E1D284-5D88-459C-83FD-6345B39B19EC" Level="5" />

    <!-- Device arrival ordering, to diff against Linux probe order. -->
    <EventProvider Id="EP_KernelPnp"   Name="9C205A39-1250-487D-ABD7-E831C6290539" Level="5" />

    <!-- Qualcomm's own subsystem driver. WPP-backed, so payloads may need the
         format strings mined out of the .sys files; included because the event
         timing alone is useful even when the payload is opaque. -->
    <EventProvider Id="EP_QcomSubsys"  Name="6312E0ED-E7A9-4667-B03D-308F80D13A3B" Level="5" />

    <Profile Id="SP11Platform.Verbose.File"
             Name="SP11Platform"
             Description="SP11 bring-up: USB4, Type-C, PnP, Qualcomm subsystem"
             LoggingMode="File"
             DetailLevel="Verbose">
      <Collectors Operation="Add">
        <SystemCollectorId Value="SystemCollector_SP11">
          <SystemProviderId Value="SystemProvider_SP11" />
        </SystemCollectorId>
        <EventCollectorId Value="EventCollector_SP11">
          <EventProviders Operation="Add">
            <EventProviderId Value="EP_UcmUcsiCx" />
            <EventProviderId Value="EP_Usb4Router" />
            <EventProviderId Value="EP_UsbHub3" />
            <EventProviderId Value="EP_UsbXhci" />
            <EventProviderId Value="EP_KernelPnp" />
            <EventProviderId Value="EP_QcomSubsys" />
          </EventProviders>
        </EventCollectorId>
      </Collectors>
    </Profile>

    <!-- WPR requires a Light detail level to exist alongside Verbose. -->
    <Profile Id="SP11Platform.Light.File"
             Name="SP11Platform"
             Description="SP11 bring-up: USB4, Type-C, PnP, Qualcomm subsystem"
             Base="SP11Platform.Verbose.File"
             LoggingMode="File"
             DetailLevel="Light" />

  </Profiles>
</WindowsPerformanceRecorder>
